' type='text/html' href='../../../2016/09/some-good-land-navigation-info.html?showComment=1473772184735#c420900330313117212' title=''/><author><name>Eddy Matthews</name><uri>http://www.blogger.com/profile/04727005486614250003</uri><email>noreply@blogger.com</email><gd:image rel='http://schemas.google.com/g/2005#thumbnail' width='16' height='16' src='http://img1.blogblog.com/img/b16-rounded.gif'/></author><thr:in-reply-to xmlns:thr='http://purl.org/syndication/thread/1.0' href='/sipseystreetirregulars.blogspot.com/2016/09/some-good-land-navigation-info.html' ref='tag:blogger.com,1999:blog-7575061201749703300.post-1476191557544014681' source='http://www.blogger.com/feeds/7575061201749703300/posts/default/1476191557544014681' type='text/html'/><gd:extendedProperty name="blogger.itemClass" value="pid-291951394"/><gd:extendedProperty name="blogger.displayTime" value="September 13, 2016 at 8:09 AM"/></entry><entry><id>tag:blogger.com,1999:blog-7575061201749703300.post-6251379884294266928</id><published>2016-09-13T04:30:41.755-05:00</published><updated>2016-09-13T04:30:41.755-05:00</updated><title type='text'>Having never been in the military but having a hug...</title><content type='html'>Having never been in the military but having a huge interest in land navigation, I learned a great deal from this Army publication   https://fas.org/irp/doddir/army/fm3-25-26.pdf     In upstate NY we have tons of hiking trails through state park areas like Harriman, Shunemunk mountain, Appalachian Trail, and the list goes on and on. I hike some using topographic maps and a compass. Of course being on the trail makes it easy but it&amp;#39;s good practice anyway for learning the basics of locating exactly where you are on the map, what direction you&amp;#39;re heading and so forth. Idiots do get lost on some of these trails occasionally, like the Hasidic teens lost on Bear Mountain two weeks ago. This isn&amp;#39;t exactly Alaska, so how anyone can get lost here is beyond me, but it does happen. Another site I&amp;#39;ve found is this one that will make a topo map of the A.O. you need   http://www.mytopo.com/index.cfm </content><link rel='edit' type='application/atom+xml' href='http://www.blogger.com/feeds/7575061201749703300/1476191557544014681/comments/default/6251379884294266928'/><link rel='self' type='application/atom+xml' href='http://www.blogger.com/feeds/7575061201749703300/1476191557544014681/comments/default/6251379884294266928'/><link rel='alternate' type='text/html' href='../../../2016/09/some-good-land-navigation-info.html?showComment=1473759041755#c6251379884294266928' title=''/><author><name>Anonymous</name><email>noreply@blogger.com</email><gd:image rel='http://schemas.google.com/g/2005#thumbnail' width='16' height='16' src='http://img1.blogblog.com/img/blank.gif'/></author><thr:in-reply-to xmlns:thr='http://purl.org/syndication/thread/1.0' href='/sipseystreetirregulars.blogspot.com/2016/09/some-good-land-navigation-info.html' ref='tag:blogger.com,1999:blog-7575061201749703300.post-1476191557544014681' source='http://www.blogger.com/feeds/7575061201749703300/posts/default/1476191557544014681' type='text/html'/><gd:extendedProperty name="blogger.itemClass" value="pid-1464333804"/><gd:extendedProperty name="blogger.displayTime" value="September 13, 2016 at 4:30 AM"/></entry><entry><id>tag:blogger.com,1999:blog-7575061201749703300.post-7780502553082624753</id><published>2016-09-13T00:59:06.211-05:00</published><updated>2016-09-13T00:59:06.211-05:00</updated><title type='text'>If only we could get DMA MGRS maps for civilian us...</title><content type='html'>If only we could get DMA MGRS maps for civilian use, instead of craptastic USGS degree-based maps. :/&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;</content><link rel='edit' type='application/atom+xml' href='http://www.blogger.com/feeds/7575061201749703300/1476191557544014681/comments/default/7780502553082624753'/><link rel='self' type='application/atom+xml' href='http://www.blogger.com/feeds/7575061201749